\chapter{Computational Methodology}
\label{ch:Methodology}

\noindent The H\"uckel model was implemented in Python using NumPy and SciPy for numerical calculations, Matplotlib for 2D plotting and PyVista for 3D molecular orbital visualisation. The program was organised into reusable functions for lattice generation, Hamiltonian construction, eigenvalue analysis and graphical output.


\section{Lattice Construction}

\noindent Finite graphene structures were generated by constructing a central benzene ring and adding successive shells of neighbouring hexagons. Carbon positions were calculated using a fixed nearest neighbour bond length of $a_{\mathrm{CC}}=1.42$~\AA, with duplicate atomic positions removed to preserve the honeycomb lattice. Completed shells contain $N=6m^2$ carbon atoms, where $m$ denotes the number of completed shells. Intermediate structures were generated by partially filling the next outer ring until the requested site count was reached. These geometries were not subjected to structural optimisation and therefore represent idealised carbon networks. \\

\noindent Nearest neighbour bonds were identified from the atomic coordinates using a spatial search with a bond length tolerance of $0.10$~\AA. The resulting connectivity was used to construct the symmetric H\"uckel Hamiltonian, with diagonal elements $\alpha=0$ and nonzero off diagonal elements $\beta=-1$ for bonded carbon pairs.


\section{Electronic Structure Calculations}

\noindent The Hamiltonian was diagonalised numerically to obtain the molecular orbital energies and eigenvectors. For even $N$, the HOMO and LUMO were identified from the two central eigenvalues, and their separation was converted to electronvolts using $|\beta|=2.40$ eV. For calculations requiring only the energy gap, SciPy was used to evaluate these two eigenvalues without computing the complete set of eigenvectors, reducing computational expense. The underlying methodology and original implementation were developed by the author, while ChatGPT was consulted to identify and implement computational optimisations. \\

\noindent The HOMO-LUMO gap was evaluated for complete shell structures up to $N=294$ and for intermediate even site counts over the same range. Calculated gaps were reused across the comparison plots to avoid redundant diagonalisation. The complete shell results were additionally fitted to a power law, $\Delta E_{\mathrm{HL}}\propto N^{-p}$, using linear regression in logarithmic space, allowing comparison with the theoretical $N^{-1/2}$ scaling estimate.


\section{Molecular Orbital Visualisation}

\noindent The calculated eigenvectors were combined with Slater-type $2p_z$ atomic functions to reconstruct molecular orbitals on a 3D Cartesian grid. Positive and negative isosurfaces were extracted at 20\% of the maximum absolute wavefunction amplitude for each orbital and visualised using PyVista, with carbon atoms and nearest neighbour bonds superimposed. Additional orthogonal views and two-dimensional contour plots were generated to examine orbital phase and nodal structure. \\

\noindent Orbital reconstruction was performed separately from the Hamiltonian diagonalisation and did not affect the calculated eigenvalues. Selected visualisations of benzene and coronene were used to illustrate the spatial characteristics of bonding, antibonding and frontier molecular orbitals.
